{
  "id": "Compound::DB09028 - Gene::1136",
  "verbalization": "Cytisine, through its interaction with Chrnb4, appears to modulate the activity of nicotinic acetylcholine receptors (nAChRs), as evidenced by its binding to Chrnb4 and the subsequent participation of Chrnb4 in different receptor subtypes, including highly sodium permeable and highly calcium permeable nicotinic acetylcholine receptors. These receptor subtypes are all part of the broader family of nAChRs, which are critical for neurotransmission and are implicated in various physiological and pathological processes. The integration of these pathways suggests that Cytisine may exert its effects by influencing the function of nAChRs, potentially through interactions that alter receptor sensitivity or activity. The evidence from the three paths highlights a consistent mechanism involving the binding of Cytisine to Chrnb4, which then participates in the regulation of nAChR activity, ultimately leading to a potential impact on the function of Chrna3. The relationship is supported by the shared pathway of participation in nAChR subtypes, indicating a possible role in modulating receptor function across different subtypes.",
